<section class="wrap band" id="scope">
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Scope · SI 2026/615, regulation 3</p>
      <h2>What has to be reported, and what doesn’t.</h2>
      <p class="lede">The regime targets control short of ownership. Four tests decide whether a right is caught, six narrow exemptions take some out, and the guidance says to read the exemptions restrictively: anything with a development purpose is probably in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tests rv">
      <div><span class="k">Test 1</span><h4>Registered land</h4><p>England and Wales. Registered freehold, or registered leasehold with more than 15 years unexpired when the right is granted. Unregistered land is outside the regime.</p></div>
      <div><span class="k">Test 2</span><h4>A contractual control right</h4><p>An option, a conditional contract, a pre-emption right, or a right under a promotion agreement that controls how the land is used, developed or disposed of. Absolute or conditional, whole or part of a title.</p></div>
      <div><span class="k">Test 3</span><h4>Held for an undertaking</h4><p>The grantee holds the right for the purposes of a business, charity or other organised activity. A private individual buying a neighbour’s paddock for themselves is not caught.</p></div>
      <div><span class="k">Test 4</span><h4>18 months or more</h4><p>The total period of control, including extensions, is at least 18 months. Shorter rights are exempt, which is why 17-month options are about to become popular and why HMLR says it will look at substance.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <h3>Caught <span class="pill">In scope</span></h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li><span><strong>Option agreements</strong> giving the grantee the right to call for a transfer or a lease of 15 years or more.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Conditional contracts</strong>, typically conditional on planning, where completion can be required by the grantee.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Pre-emption rights</strong>: rights of first refusal over a future disposal.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Promotion agreement rights</strong> where the promoter can control use, development or sale, including hybrid promotion-and-option structures.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Later events</strong> on any of the above from 6 April 2027: assignment to a new party, a written variation that changes the type, scope, land, duration or exercise conditions, exercise, and expiry or determination where the right was previously reported.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Older agreements</strong> signed before 8 June 2026 are pulled in by any of those later events.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Exempt <span class="pill out">Read narrowly</span></h3>
        <ul class="list out">
          <li><span><strong>Security.</strong> Rights granted solely to secure a loan or other financial instrument, and security for overage owed to a former owner.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Short leases.</strong> Leasehold estates with 15 years or less to run when the right is granted.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Non-development rights.</strong> Rights granted exclusively for purposes other than development. The guidance warns that a right which in substance relates to future development stays in scope however it is drafted.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Short-lived rights.</strong> Total control period under 18 months.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Section 106 rights.</strong> Rights relating exclusively to infrastructure, amenities or services tied to a planning permission.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>National security or defence</strong> contracts, and agreements made to facilitate or finance an in-scope agreement.</span></li>
          <li><span><strong>Ordinary transactions.</strong> Sales, leases, mortgages, and overage or clawback deals in their own right, which were excluded after consultation.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="head" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)">
      <p class="eyebrow">What goes in the submission · parts 3 and 5 of the guidance</p>
      <h2>Ten things per right.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Collected once at grant, then again on every assignment, variation or exercise. Items marked ∗ are verified but never published.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fields rv">
      <div><p class="k">Parties</p><p class="v">Grantor and grantee</p><p class="d">Full names of both. Criminal liability for the submission sits with the grantee.</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Identifiers</p><p class="v">Company or charity number</p><p class="d">Companies House or Charity Commission number for any organisation. This is how the dataset resolves who really holds control.</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Individuals ∗</p><p class="v">Date and place of birth</p><p class="d">Required where the grantor is a person, for identity verification only. Not published.</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Right</p><p class="v">Type of right</p><p class="d">Option, conditional contract, pre-emption, promotion. Plus the date, parties and title of the underlying agreement.</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Exercise</p><p class="v">When, or on what conditions</p><p class="d">The date the right can be exercised, or the trigger (typically grant of planning).</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Duration</p><p class="v">Control period</p><p class="d">The initial period and every extension, renewal or termination provision. This is what powers expiry intelligence from 2028.</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Land</p><p class="v">Title numbers</p><p class="d">Every registered title affected, and whether the whole or part. Part-title rights need machine-readable geometry: GeoJSON or Shapefile on British National Grid, not a scanned plan.</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Address</p><p class="v">Address and postcode</p><p class="d">Of the land, for the geographic search HMLR is building the dataset around.</p></div>
      <div><p class="k">Strata</p><p class="v">Airspace and sub-surface</p><p class="d">Whether the land includes airspace or subterranean estates held separately from the surface.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="conseq rv">
      <div class="c1"><p class="eyebrow">If it isn’t reported</p><h3>Two consequences, one of them criminal.</h3></div>
      <div class="c2"><p><strong>Section 225, Levelling-up and Regeneration Act 2023.</strong> Failing to provide the information, or knowingly or recklessly providing false or misleading information, is an offence. It applies to the grantee, and to companies, partnerships and the officers acting for them. An indemnity from the landowner does not move the liability.</p></div>
      <div class="c3"><p><strong>No protection on the register.</strong> HM Land Registry may refuse to enter a notice or restriction protecting a right until the information has been provided. For a developer that is the difference between an option that binds a buyer and one that doesn’t.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="head" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)">
      <p class="eyebrow">Primary documents</p>
      <h2>Read the sources, not the summaries.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Everything on this page comes from these. We re-read them whenever HMLR changes anything, and the news stream will say when they do.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs rv">
      <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/615"><span class="k">Statutory instrument</span><strong>SI 2026/615: The Provision of Information (Contractual Control) (Registered Land) Regulations 2026</strong><span>Made 8 June 2026, in force 6 April 2027. Regulation 3 defines the rights; the schedule lists the information.</span></a>
      <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2023/55/section/221/2023-12-26"><span class="k">Primary legislation</span><strong>Levelling-up and Regeneration Act 2023, Part 11, sections 221 to 225</strong><span>The powers to require and publish contractual control information, and the section 225 offence.</span></a>
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/contractual-control-agreements/contractual-control-agreements"><span class="k">MHCLG guidance</span><strong>Contractual control agreements: guidance for developers, landowners and conveyancers</strong><span>Non-statutory. Who must comply, what to provide, when, and how the data is published. Published 9 March 2026.</span></a>
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/hm-land-registry-new-reporting-rules-for-contractual-control-rights"><span class="k">HM Land Registry</span><strong>New reporting rules for contractual control rights</strong><span>HMLR’s own note for conveyancers: timetable, the digital service, spatial data formats, the practice guide to come.</span></a>
      <a href="https://hmlandregistry.blog.gov.uk/2026/09/17/help-shape-our-new-contractual-control-service/"><span class="k">HMLR blog · Sept 2026</span><strong>Help shape our new contractual control service</strong><span>What user research has changed in the submission service, and how to take part.</span></a>
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/contractual-controls-on-land-consultation/outcome/contractual-controls-on-land-consultation-government-response"><span class="k">Consultation response</span><strong>Contractual controls on land: government response</strong><span>Why overage and unregistered land were left out, and what respondents said about confidentiality.</span></a>
      <a href="https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/65b1289ff2718c000dfb1c95/Contractual_controls_consultation_stage_impact_assessment.pdf"><span class="k">Impact assessment · PDF</span><strong>Contractual controls consultation stage impact assessment</strong><span>The government’s own estimate of agreement volumes, costs to firms and HMLR, and the options it rejected.</span></a>
      <a href="https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/65b128a8f2718c000dfb1c96/Land_Transparency_SI.pdf"><span class="k">Draft SI · PDF</span><strong>Land Transparency SI, consultation draft (Jan 2024)</strong><span>The earlier draft. Useful for seeing what changed before the regulations were made.</span></a>
      <a href="https://api.parliament.uk/procedure/WorkPackages/7716"><span class="k">Parliament</span><strong>Procedural record: laying and approval of the Regulations</strong><span>Dates laid, committee consideration, and approval in both Houses.</span></a>
    </div>
    <div class="head" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)">
      <p class="eyebrow">Commentary</p>
      <h2>What the law firms are telling their clients.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="docs rv firms">
      <a href="https://www.brownejacobson.com/insights/new-transparency-rules-for-contractual-control-rights-over-registered-land-guide-for-developers"><span class="k">Browne Jacobson</span><strong>Guide for developers</strong><span>Confidentiality carve-outs, who owns each disclosure event, budgeting conveyancer costs.</span></a>
